WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:09.140
As we mentioned in the previous slide, optimization starts at the beginning of your project and is something that goes through the lifetime of your project.

2
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:15.140
So making sure that your project is set up from the initial creation with optimization in mind

3
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:22.140
is key to making sure that you will have a successful optimization throughout the life of your project.

4
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:29.140
One of the first areas to look at is under Project Settings and it is the Rendering section.

5
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:36.140
Under this section, you're going to find a wide range of options that directly affect both performance and visual quality.

6
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:46.140
This is where we're going to be able to control features such as rendering paths, ray tracing, virtual textures, and shadow maps, and many other features here.

7
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:54.140
For example, we could enable or disable forward renderers should we want to have our project use less memory and a lower overhead.

8
00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:59.140
We can also use this section to disable hardware or software ray tracing.

9
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:05.140
We can also use it to tune how virtual textures and shadow maps behave.

10
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:17.140
And we can even disable things like shader permutations for skylights or sky atmosphere or other types of lights or assets we don't happen to be using

11
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:24.140
which can reduce shader compile time and free up memory without affecting our visuals.

12
00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:34.140
But again, we want to make sure that we do this from the beginning of our project so that we don't have any unexpected visual changes after enabling or disabling a feature.

13
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:42.680
To go into a few of these features in a little more detail, the first one I want to talk about is virtual shadows and textures.

14
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:49.680
Now, virtual texture support is enabled by default in Unreal Engine 5.5.

15
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:57.680
If you're working with Unreal Engine 5.4 all the way to 5.0, you will need to enable virtual texture support and restart the engine.

16
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:08.680
If you're not aware, what virtual texturing support allows you to do is use very large textures while still maintaining a lower and more consistent memory footprint at runtime.

17
00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:19.780
This system also helps improve texture streaming efficiency, reducing memory spikes while still maintaining high quality visuals across large environments.

18
00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:27.780
There are two key types of virtual texturing to understand. There's runtime virtual texturing and streaming virtual texturing.

19
00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:35.780
Runtime virtual texturing is used whenever you have a very large layered material such as one that would go on a landscape.

20
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:43.780
What you're going to do is we're going to take all those layers and we're going to bake them down to a single texture and then display that as the virtual texture on our landscape.

21
00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:50.780
This is going to help us just have a more efficient memory footprint as well as maintaining that really great visual look.

22
00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.780
The other type is streaming virtual textures.

23
00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:59.780
Streaming virtual textures replace the built-in MIPT based streaming system.

24
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:12.780
All you have to do to support streaming based is make sure that your textures are converted to virtual textures and that you are using the appropriate virtual texture sampler inside of your materials.

25
00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.379
The next thing that I want to talk about here is virtual shadow maps.

26
00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:23.379
The best part about virtual shadow maps, there's not a lot that you need to do with them as they're already enabled by default.

27
00:03:23.379 --> 00:03:31.379
And what they are going to do is they're going to give us this increased shadow resolution so that shadows match the high level of detail provided by Nanette geometry.

28
00:03:31.379 --> 00:03:38.379
They also help produce plausible soft shadows with predictable and controllable performance costs.

29
00:03:38.379 --> 00:03:45.610
They also work extremely well out of the box requiring minimal adjustments to achieve good results.

30
00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:54.180
Now, one thing that I would like to call out when using virtual shadows is that if your project uses a fully dynamic time of day system,

31
00:03:54.180 --> 00:04:02.180
you should disable the variable r.shadows.virtual.cache.static separate.

32
00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:08.180
This setting is on by default, but disabling it saves memory because dynamic shadows will not need a static copy.

33
00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:17.180
Turning this off can reduce shadow memory uses by roughly half, which is especially helpful for large open worlds or streaming environments.

34
00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:25.790
Now, the next section we're going to talk about is Lumen and Lumen is Unreal Engine 5's real time global illumination and reflection system.

35
00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:30.790
It provides a high quality lighting without the need for baking.

36
00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:39.790
It is enabled by default for all new projects in Unreal Engine 5 and works best when used together with World Partition, Nanite, and virtual shadow maps.

37
00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:47.879
Lumen supports two ray tracing modes, hardware ray tracing and software ray tracing.

38
00:04:47.879 --> 00:04:59.879
Hardware ray tracing is on by default. It uses a GPU's dedicated ray tracing cores and it's meant for high-end PCs and Gen 9 consoles such as the PlayStation 5 and the Xbox Siri X.

39
00:04:59.879 --> 00:05:16.379
If you do enable it in your projects, you can expect around a 4 millisecond time and it can now maintain a steady 60 frames per second on high-end PCs and Gen 9 consoles.

40
00:05:16.379 --> 00:05:25.379
Software ray tracing, on the other hand, uses compute shaders instead of hardware acceleration and it serves as a fallback for mid-range GPUs.

41
00:05:25.379 --> 00:05:44.980
It also runs at around 4 milliseconds on average and, again, we're using this as a fallback or for systems that don't necessarily have hardware ray tracing so expect it to not be as powerful or as accurate.

42
00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:56.980
Starting with Unreal Engine 5.3, we have a few 60 FPS frame per second default device profiles which enable Lumen with all of the settings it needs to hit that 60 FPS.

43
00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:05.620
These are going to be the PC60 for high-end PCs, PlayStation 5 and Xbox Series X.

44
00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:12.620
These profiles are designed to help developers hit that constant 60 FPS targets with both Lumen and Nanite available.

45
00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:25.620
And it's recommended to move all Gen 9 console projects and high-end PC projects to hardware ray tracing and you should keep software ray tracing on for less capable or older hardware.

46
00:06:26.709 --> 00:06:31.709
Again, for the best results, always use Lumen alongside Nanite, World Partition and Virtual Shadow Maps.

47
00:06:31.709 --> 00:06:38.709
And make sure to profile performance regularly using Unreal Insights, Unreal's built-in performance profiling tool.
